"""English colophon and page labels."""

LABELS = {'colophon': 'The text used, and how it was established'}

COLOPHON = [
 'The <b>base text</b> is the punctuated <i>Sanlüe</i> at Chinese Wikisource — three scrolls, Upper, Middle and Lower, 3,815 characters of text. The text on this page runs to 3,816, because one omitted character has been supplied (the punctuation added here is not counted).',
 'It was <b>collated character by character against two witnesses</b>. The first is the unpunctuated plain text of the <i>Three Strategies of Huang Shigong</i> in the <i>Complete Library of the Four Treasuries</i> (Siku quanshu). The second is the classic text carried inside Liu Yin’s <i>Direct Explanation of the Three Strategies</i>, a Ming commentary also taken into the Siku — Liu Yin’s text descends from the <i>Seven Military Classics</i> promulgated in the Ming and is not the same line of transmission as the plain text, so the two can check one another. Between them they yield <b>sixty-two variant readings</b> (forty-six in the plain text, forty-eight in Liu Yin, thirty-two of them shared), besides some three hundred and forty differences of old graphic form and interchangeable usage, which are not counted as variants. Graphic differences are judged by four CJKVI variant tables (cjkvi-variants, twedu-variants, hydzd-variants, jp-old-style), and only pairs listed directly in them are accepted, with no transitive closure — those tables contain entries such as 上/二 and 丈/支, and a few steps of union-find pulls unrelated characters into the same class.',
 'A <b>third witness was consulted by eye</b>: the <b>Fushimi movable-type printing of 1604</b> held at the National Diet Library of Japan (shelfmark WA7-22), whose opening leaf is this page’s cover. It was cut by order of Tokugawa Ieyasu, and its own colophon says so: “The above <i>Three Strategies</i> was cut and printed by order of the Shogun Ieyasu. Errors in the blocks were corrected from the two commentaries, the <i>Exposition</i> and the <i>Direct Explanation</i>.” It does not descend from the Siku line and so serves as an independent check. Being set in old movable type with Japanese reading marks, it cannot be machine-collated, so it was read only at the decisive places; what it shows is noted there.',
 '<b>Twenty-three readings in the base text were changed, and no more.</b> The rule is one sentence: <b>where the base text does not make sense, follow the two witnesses; where it does, keep it and record the variant.</b> The changes fall into four kinds. (1) <b>Seventeen artefacts of a round trip through simplified Chinese</b>: “one” written with the banker’s graph twelve times, “will” written as “record” twice, “divine” written as “radish”, “sign” written as “campaign”, “stove” written in its simplified form. This is the fingerprint of a text that was converted to simplified characters and back: the Taiwan Ministry of Education’s variant table lists exactly those pairs, and an over-eager traditionalising tool took them at face value. (2) <b>Four corruptions of similar-looking graphs.</b> (3) <b>One omitted character</b>: “troops,” in “once there was a good general who used troops.” (4) <b>One unintelligible reading</b>: “the four orders of the people run <i>numinous</i>,” where both witnesses read “run <i>empty</i>,” answering “have enough” in the next clause. In every one of the twenty-three, the plain text and Liu Yin agree against the base text; none rests on a single witness.',
 '<b>No other variant was adopted.</b> In thirty-two places both witnesses differ from the base text and the base text still makes sense — “the strong is what <i>resentment</i> attacks” (witnesses: what <i>men</i> attack), “those who submit, <i>settle</i> them” (witnesses: <i>spare their lives</i>), “the men are careless of <i>death</i>” (witnesses: of <i>battle</i>), “his ministers yield the credit” (witnesses: <i>ruler and ministers</i>), “without <i>secret scheming</i> there is no success” (witnesses: <i>secret reckoning</i>), “rulers of the <i>age</i> keep it secret” (witnesses: rulers of <i>power</i>). All are kept as they stand, with the variant noted on the passage.',
 '<b>One place has three witnesses and two readings, and both are given.</b> At the head of the book, “the weak is what men will help; the strong is what resentment attacks,” the Siku plain text and Liu Yin both read “what men attack,” while the Fushimi printing reads “resentment” — and carries on its opening leaf a later hand’s small marginal note, “<b>the <i>Exposition</i> has <i>resentment</i>, the <i>Direct Explanation</i> has <i>men</i></b>.” A Japanese reader had written down this variant four hundred years ago, and what he wrote agrees exactly with what the machine collation finds. The base reading is kept and both are recorded.',
 '<b>The division into passages is not this page’s; it is Liu Yin’s.</b> The <i>Three Strategies</i> has no chapters and no sections: each of its three scrolls is one continuous stretch of prose, and the base text, though punctuated, is not broken into paragraphs at all. Liu Yin’s <i>Direct Explanation</i> comments section by section, and where his comment falls is where he judged a section to end. This page takes those breaks as its paragraph breaks — sixty-six in the Upper, twenty-one in the Middle, thirty-four in the Lower, a hundred and twenty-one in all, which come out here as <b>a hundred and nineteen paragraphs</b> (two breaks in the Upper Strategy fall where the base text offers no punctuation to cut at, and those sections are joined to the one before). Not a character of the text has been added or removed.',
 '<b>There are no section headings on this page.</b> The only divisions the book itself has are Upper, Middle and Lower; there are no chapter titles and no numbered sections. So the table of contents has three entries and this one. Headings of the sort “On Seeking the Worthy” or “On Generalship” appear in no transmitted edition, and none has been composed here.',
 '<b>About the book’s parentage.</b> It is ascribed to Huang Shigong, the Yellow Stone Elder. The <i>Records of the Historian</i> tells how Zhang Liang met an old man on the bridge at Xiapi who gave him a roll of writing, saying “read this and you will be teacher to kings,” and that in the morning he found it was <i>the Grand Duke’s Art of War</i> — the <i>Records</i> names that book, not this one. The Han imperial bibliography does not list a <i>Three Strategies</i>; <b>the title first appears in the Sui bibliography</b>, entered as “by the divine man of Xiapi, with a commentary by Mr Cheng.” The Qing imperial catalogue concluded from this that the book is a later forgery, and quoted Zheng Yuan’s <i>Jingguan suoyan</i>: “pure plagiarism from Laozi, slow and rambling, of no practical use.” The same catalogue entry, however, records the case on the other side — the Song writer Dai Shaowang held that the <i>Three Strategies</i> “reaches the Way and is fit for use, and by it a man may achieve something and still keep himself”; Zhen Dexiu held that what it says about governing a state and nourishing the people “does not run counter to what the Confucians mean”; and <b>an edict of Emperor Guangwu of AD 51, preserved in the biography of Zang Gong in the <i>History of the Later Han</i>, quotes this book at length under the name <i>Record of the Yellow Stone Elder</i></b>. What it quotes is not one sentence. After “the soft is virtue, the hard is injury, the weak is what humaneness helps, the strong is what resentment settles upon” it goes on: “the lord who has virtue uses what he delights in to gladden others; the lord who has none uses it to gladden himself…”; “to leave what is near and plan for what is far is labour without result…”; “he whose whole aim is to enlarge his territory lays it waste…” — <b>three consecutive passages of the present <i>Lower Strategy</i>, in exactly that order</b>. A book whose wording the present <i>Three Strategies</i> continues was therefore in circulation by the middle of the first century, and that is the hardest piece of external evidence there is. <b>It also settles one variant</b>: the edict reads “the strong is what <i>resentment</i> settles upon,” agreeing with the base text’s “resentment” against the Siku text and Liu Yin, who read “men” — which is why this page keeps the base reading. The catalogue ends: “where the doubt is handed down, let the doubt stand; we let it pass and preserve it.” That is probably still the safest thing to say. Under the Song, when a military curriculum was established in the Yuanfeng period, the <i>Three Strategies</i> was made one of the <i>Seven Military Classics</i>.',
 'The reading above is a complete translation, passage for passage, not a summary; the scroll headings and the parenthetical notes are this page’s own.',
]
